(def vec-page "(defmacro vec (elements ...))\n\nBuilds a vector from its elements, which all have one type.\n\n`(vec)` is empty. `vec` is also the type `(vec a)`, Rust's `Vec<A>`.")
(def iter-page "(iter collection)\n\nReturns an iterator over borrows of a collection's items.\n\nA vector or a slice yields `(ref a)`, and a map yields\n`(pair (ref key) (ref value))`. Where a call expects an `iterator`, a vector,\na slice or a map passes without `iter`.")
(def sort-page "(sort! items)\n\nSorts a vector in place, in ascending order.\n\n`(sort! items <)` does the same, and `(sort! items >)` sorts in descending\norder. Both need items that implement `ord`.\n\n`(sort! items compare)` orders by a function of two `(ref a)` that returns\nan `i64`. A negative result puts the first item first, a positive result\nputs it last, and zero keeps the two in order. Rust's `sort_by` may panic\nwhen `compare` is not a consistent order.")
(def windows-page "(windows items size)\n\nReturns an iterator over each run of `size` neighboring items of a slice.\n\nEach run is a `(ref (slice a))`. `size` is a `usize`, so an `i64` is an\nerror. A slice shorter than `size` gives no run. It panics when `size` is\nzero. A vector passes as its slice.")
